/**

@page benchmarks_exagrype_gauge_wave Gauge wave (ExaGRyPE Python API)

The gauge wave is a pure gauge perturbation of flat spacetime that travels
along the x-axis with speed one. It is an analytical, periodic solution of the
Einstein equations over the unit cube and hence the standard first test of a
numerical relativity code.

This benchmark sets up the gauge wave with exagrype.api.CCZ4Solver and
discretises it with the Finite Volume scheme of exahype2.api. It is the
reference example of the ExaGRyPE Python API (@ref page_exagrype_library). The
older benchmark @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_gauge_wave uses the legacy API.

# Build and run

Configure Peano with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and build it. Then, within this directory,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 gauge-wave.py
./gauge-wave
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The script builds a small debug version by default. Set
\c create_release_build to True for a finer mesh and a run until t=1.

# Fourth-order Finite Differences

The production runs of ExaGRyPE use the fourth-order Finite Differences of
@ref exahype2.api.rkfd4 rather than Finite Volumes. To use them, replace the
numerical scheme, the grid construction and the plotter in gauge-wave.py by
exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta,
exahype2.api.rkfd4.SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid and
exahype2.api.rkfd4.PlotAsVTKFile. With 27 grid cells per axis, the maximum
error of the lapse after one period (t=1) is roughly 5e-5 with the
fourth-order Finite Differences (Runge-Kutta of order four, no dissipation),
compared to roughly 4e-2 with the Finite Volumes of exahype2.api.fv.

# Limitations

There is no constraint enforcement after the time steps yet, i.e. the
solution is not pushed back onto the CCZ4 constraint surface. Long runs hence
drift away from it.

*/
